# hostile plan: seed=0 ops=4
#
# A SECOND WRITABLE SESSION OVER AN ADOPTED FILE. Found by the
# second-session phase this vocabulary added (at 8a97e58), filed unfixed,
# and FIXED by readopt-agent -- so this entry is now a regression that must
# PASS, and the marker it used to carry is gone. What it pins:
#
#     ERROR pelfs: open the write path's content store:
#     memtable: re-adopt inode 3: genfs: stale inode (no residency)
#
# `pelfs mount-gen --rw` exited 1 before mounting, and not for one file:
# the volume could not be mounted for writing from that state directory
# again, by this harness or by a user. Both frontends, every run.
#
# THE SEQUENCE, and it is four operations:
#   1. write a file
#   2. let a CHECKPOINT publish it, so it is in a base generation
#   3. overwrite part of it -- which makes the memtable ADOPT the file
#      from that base by reference (memtable.Store.Adopt), journalling an
#      adopted handle against the generation it adopted from
#   4. let a SECOND checkpoint publish, and seal
# Then mount the same state directory --rw again. Recovery replayed the
# journal and re-adopted every adopted handle against whatever base the
# mount now had (memtable/recover.go, `Adopted extents come back from the
# base, which still describes those files exactly as it did when they were
# taken over`). Two checkpoints later that comment was no longer true and
# ContentOf refused -- a freshly opened generation answers for an inode
# only after a descent has made it resident, and a mount that has just
# started has descended nothing.
#
# WHAT IS AND IS NOT LOAD-BEARING, measured one variant at a time:
#   - the SECOND checkpoint is required. Drop the last settle and the same
#     plan passed even at 8a97e58: one checkpoint, one adoption, no reopen
#     failure. What the second one does is REBASE the published inode
#     clean, which Forgets its content -- so the handle recovery refused
#     over was one that no surviving content row named, and that it would
#     have deleted as dead-on-arrival three lines later.
#   - the FILL IS IRRELEVANT. `fill=41` reproduced it identically. It is
#     written as fill=text here for one mechanical reason: phase C2 picks
#     its rewrite targets from files the plan wrote with a compressible
#     body, because for an incompressible one a re-chunked row is
#     accidentally correct and proves nothing. So the fill selects the
#     file, and nothing else.
#   - no deletion was needed. unlink, rename and truncate-to-zero of the
#     adopted file all reproduced it, and so did leaving it alone, which
#     is what made the four-op form findable.
#   - both frontends, deterministically. On FUSE it fired twice per run
#     (phase C2 and phase E, which also reopens the state directory); on
#     NFS once, because phase E is FUSE-only by design.
#
# WHY NO GATE HAD SEEN IT: nothing else ever ran a second WRITABLE session
# over a generation a previous one published. Phase E reopens the state
# directory, but on a plan that had no adoption in it, and the old
# vocabulary had no shape that reliably produced checkpoint-then-partial-
# overwrite -- which is the only way an adoption happens.
#
# THE FIX, in one line each, and both are pinned by ordinary-lane tests in
# internal/memtable and internal/publish (see docs/TODO.md, readopt-agent):
# an adopted handle no surviving row names is never resolved at all, and
# the records of one that IS named come from the journal, which records
# them at adoption time instead of asking a base that has moved on.
mkdir ad mode=0755
create ad/a.log len=40000 fill=text:11 # the body a checkpoint will publish
settle ms=1100 # checkpoint: now it is in a base generation
pwrite ad/a.log off=101 len=999 fill=text:21 # partial overwrite -> the memtable ADOPTS from that base
settle ms=1100 # a second checkpoint, which moves the base out from under the journal
compare
